        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "429202",
          "text": "A relational scheme is in..... if it is in 1NF and if all non prime attributes are fully functionally dependent on the relation key<br /><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li> Second Normal Form</li><li> Boyce Codd Normal Form</li> <li>Fourth Normal Form</li>  <li>First Normal Form</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "a",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "goclasses2025_cs_da_wq14,goclasses,databases,one-mark",
          "category": "Databases"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "429197",
          "text": "<p>Let relation $R(A, B, C, D)$ satisfy the following functional dependencies:<br>$A \\rightarrow B, B \\rightarrow C, C \\rightarrow A$<br>Call this set S1. A different set S2 of functional dependencies is equivalent to S1 if exactly the same FDs follow from S1 and S2. Which of the following sets of FDs is equivalent to the set above?<br> </p><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li>$\\mathrm{B} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{A}, \\mathrm{B} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{C}, \\mathrm{C} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{B}$</li><li>$\\mathrm{A} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{BC}, \\mathrm{C} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{AB} $</li><li>$\\mathrm{A} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{BC}, \\mathrm{B} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{AC} $</li><li>$\\mathrm{C} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{B}, \\mathrm{B} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{A}, \\mathrm{A} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{C}$</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Select",
          "answer": "d",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "goclasses2025_cs_da_wq14,goclasses,databases,multiple-selects,two-marks",
          "category": "Databases"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "429191",
          "text": "Suppose relation $R(A, B, C, D, E)$ has the functional dependencies:<br />$\\mathrm{A} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{B} ; \\mathrm{BC} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{D} ; \\mathrm{BE} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{C} ; \\mathrm{AD} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{E} ; \\mathrm{CE} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{A}$<br />Let the number of candidate keys of R be M .<br />Of the five given FDs, let the number of FDs that violate the BCNF condition be N .<br />What is $\\mathrm{M}+\\mathrm{N}$ ??",
          "type": "Numerical",
          "answer": "7",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "goclasses2025_cs_da_wq14,numerical-answers,goclasses,databases,two-marks",
          "category": "Databases"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "429196",
          "text": "<p><span style=\"display: none;\" id=\"cke_bm_87S\">&nbsp;</span>&nbsp;</p><p>Consider the following table</p><p><img alt=\"\" width=\"500\" height=\"139\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\"></p><p>The table is in which normal form?</p><ol style=\"list-style-type: upper-alpha;\"><li>First Normal Form</li><li>Second&nbsp;<span style=\"line-height: 20.8px;\">Normal Form</span></li><li>Third&nbsp;<span style=\"line-height: 20.8px;\">Normal Form but not BCNF</span></li><li>Third&nbsp;<span style=\"line-height: 20.8px;\">Normal Form and BCNF</span></li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "c",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "goclasses2025_cs_da_wq14,goclasses,databases,two-marks",
          "category": "Databases"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "429194",
          "text": "<p>Consider a relational table $R$ that is in $3NF$, but not in BCNF. Which one of the following statements is TRUE?</p><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\" type=\"A\"><li>$R$ has a nontrivial functional dependency $X \\rightarrow A$, where $X$ is not a superkey and $A$ is a prime attribute.</li><li>$R$ has a nontrivial functional dependency $X \\rightarrow A$, where $X$ is not a superkey and $A$ is a non-prime attribute and $X$ is&nbsp;not&nbsp;a&nbsp;proper subset of any key.</li><li>$R$ has a nontrivial functional dependency $X \\rightarrow A$, where $X$ is not a superkey and $A$ is a non-prime attribute and $X$ is&nbsp;a&nbsp;proper subset of some key</li><li>A cell in $R$ holds a set instead of an atomic value.</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "a",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "goclasses2025_cs_da_wq14,goclasses,databases,two-marks",
          "category": "Databases"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "429192",
          "text": "<p>Let $pk(R)$ denotes primary key of relation $R$. A&nbsp;many-to-one relationship that exists between two relations $R_1$ and $R_2$ can be expressed as follows:</p><ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li>&nbsp;$pk(R_2)\\rightarrow pk(R_1)$</li><li>&nbsp;$pk(R_1)\\rightarrow pk(R_2)$</li><li>$pk(R_2)\\rightarrow&nbsp; R_1&nbsp;\\cap&nbsp;R_2$</li><li>$pk(R_1)\\rightarrow&nbsp; R_1&nbsp;\\cap&nbsp;R_2$</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "b",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "goclasses2025_cs_da_wq14,goclasses,databases,two-marks",
          "category": "Databases"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "429201",
          "text": "<p>Consider a schema $R(MNPQ)$ and functional dependencies $M\\rightarrow N, P\\rightarrow Q$. Then the decomposition of&nbsp; $R$ into $R_{1} \\left (MN \\right )$ and&nbsp; $R_{2} \\left (PQ&nbsp;\\right )$ is __________.</p><ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li>Dependency preserving but not lossless join</li><li>Dependency preserving and lossless join</li><li>Lossless join but not dependency preserving</li><li>Neither dependency preserving nor lossless join.</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "a",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "goclasses2025_cs_da_wq14,goclasses,databases,one-mark",
          "category": "Databases"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "429204",
          "text": "<p>Normalization from which is based on transitive dependency is classified as:</p><ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li>First normal form.</li><li>Second normal form.</li><li>Fourth normal form.</li><li>Third normal form.</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "d",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "goclasses2025_cs_da_wq14,goclasses,databases,one-mark",
          "category": "Databases"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "429199",
          "text": "<p>Consider the following dependencies and the BOOK table in a relational database design. Determine the normal form of the given relation.</p><p>ISBN $\\rightarrow$ Title</p><p>ISBN $\\rightarrow$ Publisher</p><p>Publisher $\\rightarrow$ Address</p><ol style=\"list-style-type: upper-alpha;\"><li>First Normal Form</li><li>Second Normal Form</li><li>Third Normal Form</li><li>BCNF</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "b",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "goclasses2025_cs_da_wq14,goclasses,databases,one-mark",
          "category": "Databases"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "429206",
          "text": "<p>Which normal form is based on the concept of 'full functional dependency' is</p><ol style=\"list-style-type: upper-alpha;\"><li>First Normal Form</li><li>Second &nbsp;Normal Form</li><li>Third Normal Form</li><li>Fourth Normal Form</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "b",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "goclasses2025_cs_da_wq14,goclasses,databases,one-mark",
          "category": "Databases"
        }
      ]
    }
